## Why break;?
You may be thinking 'Why do I need to put break after each switch case?'
This is because of something called _**Fallthrough**_. Fallthrough means that switch will keep going to the next case until it hits a break statement. In C#, instead of implicit fallthrough, it Compile Errors on missing break.


---
## Question:
What would be the output of this switch statement?
```csharp
int thing = 124;
switch(thing) {
    case 23:
        Console.WriteLine("Case 1!");
        break;
    case 124:
        Console.WriteLine("Case 2!");
    case 200:
        Console.WriteLine("Case 3!");
        break;
}
```
